<p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/10/searchlink-zen-rb.7537.jpg" width="800" height="226" style="margin:0 auto;clear:both;" class="header" /></p>
<p><a href="https://srchl.ink">SearchLink</a> can now look through <a href="https://zen-browser.app/">Zen</a> bookmarks and history the same way it already did with Firefox (and Chrome and Safari). Zen keeps its history and bookmarks in a Mozilla <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">places.sqlite</code> database, so <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">!h</code> grew a <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">z</code>.</p>

<p>If it&rsquo;s been too long and you&rsquo;ve forgotten what <a href="https://srchl.ink">SearchLink</a> is, it&rsquo;s one of my favorite projects I&rsquo;ve ever created. It lets you select any text and search for a link via a ton of sources, inserting Markdown links for everything without opening a browser. If you write in Markdown (or do a lot of show notes), you should check it out.</p>

<h3 id="the-shortcuts">The shortcuts</h3>

<p><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">!hz</code> searches both Zen history and Zen bookmarks. Add <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">h</code> or <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">b</code> when you only want one of them.</p>

<div class="language-markdown highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code><span class="p">[</span><span class="nv">jekyll</span><span class="p">](</span><span class="sx">!hzb</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="p">[</span><span class="nv">searchlink privacy</span><span class="p">](</span><span class="sx">!hzh</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="p">[</span><span class="nv">something I know I opened in Zen</span><span class="p">](</span><span class="sx">!hz</span><span class="p">)</span></code></pre></div></div>

<p>You can still stack browsers. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">!hzhfb</code> is Zen history plus Firefox bookmarks. A bare <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">!h</code> searches whatever you have turned on in the config, which now includes Zen if you enable it.</p>

<p>A search term that starts with a single quote is an exact phrase. Every other word has to show up in the URL or the title.</p>

<h3 id="updating">Updating</h3>

<p>Select the word <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">update</code> (or <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">upgrade</code>) and run the SearchLink service. On macOS that downloads the latest release and replaces the services in <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">~/Library/Services</code>. If you installed the cask, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">brew upgrade --cask searchlink</code> does the same job. The CLI build tracks the repo, so pull and you are current.</p>

<p>You can also always download the latest release <a href="https://github.com/ttscoff/searchlink/releases/latest">from GitHub</a> or below.</p>

