<h3 id="configuring-h">Configuring <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">!h</code></h3>

<p>Explicit shortcuts like <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">!hzh</code> always search Zen. The plain <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">!h</code> shortcut only searches the types listed in <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">~/.config/searchlink/config.yaml</code> (or <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">~/.searchlink</code> if you still have the old file).</p>

<p>SearchLink will not edit an existing <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">history_types</code> list for you. Add the Zen lines yourself, and comment out anything you do not want <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">!h</code> to touch:</p>

<div class="language-yaml highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code><span class="na">history_types</span><span class="pi">:</span>
  <span class="pi">-</span> <span class="s">safari_history</span>
  <span class="pi">-</span> <span class="s">safari_bookmarks</span>
  <span class="pi">-</span> <span class="s">zen_history</span>
  <span class="pi">-</span> <span class="s">zen_bookmarks</span>
  <span class="c1"># - firefox_history</span>
  <span class="c1"># - firefox_bookmarks</span></code></pre></div></div>

<h3 id="a-few-limits">A few limits</h3>

<p>This is macOS only. SearchLink reads <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">~/Library/Application Support/zen/profiles.ini</code> and uses that profile&rsquo;s <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">places.sqlite</code>. If you have more than one Zen install, it prefers the install default, then the profile marked default, and if several installs are listed it picks the database that was modified most recently. Other profiles are ignored.</p>

<p>It also needs the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">sqlite3</code> command. macOS includes that. The database is copied (including the write-ahead log) so a running Zen does not lock the query, and visits from the current session are included. What you get back is the newest matching visit or bookmark, not a ranked &ldquo;best title&rdquo; result. Search-result URLs are skipped, including Google, DuckDuckGo, Kagi, Bing, and other result pages, so a history hit should be the page you actually opened.</p>

<p>The same profile lookup is what Firefox uses now, too. Firefox used to grab the first <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">*default-release</code> folder it saw, which was the wrong profile often enough to be annoying.</p>

<h3 id="linkding">Linkding</h3>

<p>I also tightened up <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">!ld</code> (and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">!ding</code>). It used to download the whole bookmark library and search a local cache. It now asks your Linkding server, with the same query syntax as the Linkding UI: words, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">"quoted phrases"</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">#tags</code>, and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">and</code> / <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">or</code> / <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">not</code>. Archived bookmarks are included, and the link text you wrote stays put.</p>

<div class="language-yaml highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code><span class="na">linkding_server</span><span class="pi">:</span> <span class="s">https://links.example.com</span>
<span class="na">linkding_api_key</span><span class="pi">:</span> <span class="s">your-token</span></code></pre></div></div>

<p>The token is under <strong>Settings, Integrations</strong> on the server. A very broad query only looks at the first thousand matches in active bookmarks and the first thousand in the archive, then picks the best of those. An exact title or tag match wins.</p>

<p>Enjoy!</p>

